% Large-scale multilingual speech recognition models like Whisper excel in Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) for high-resource languages but struggle with adapting to low-resource scenarios, such as rare languages and code-switching (CS), due to computational costs and catastrophic forgetting. We carry out an investigation into Soft Prompt Tuning (SPT), a parameter-efficient method to enhance CS ASR while preserving prior knowledge. We evaluate SPT using two strategies: 1) fine-tuning both soft prompts and the entire Whisper model, demonstrating improved cross-lingual capabilities compared to normal fine-tuning, and 2) adhering to SPT's original intent by freezing model parameters and only training soft prompts. Additionally, we compare different SPT methods and introduce combinations of variants. Experiments on the SEAME and ASRU2019 datasets show that combined SPT methods achieve performance slightly lower than full fine-tuning and LoRA in CS ASR, but still maintain parameter efficiency and do not significantly degrade performance on other languages, proving their effectiveness. This contributes to improved continual learning in multilingual ASR.

