\section{\boldmath From $\IMLL$ to $\IBV$}\label{sec:IMLLtoIBV}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%


In this section we show that $\BiV$ is a conservative extension of intuitionistic multiplicative linear logic ($\MiLL$): 
every $\IMLL$-formula that is provable in $\IBV$ is also provable in $\IMLL$.

We assume the reader to be familiar with sequent calculi and cut-elimination.
%
A \defn{sequent} is a pair $\Gamma\vdash A$, where $\Gamma$ is a (possibly empty) set of occurrences of formulas, and $A$ is a formula.
\Cref{fig:IMLL} shows the inference rules of the sequent calculus for $\MiLL$, and it's well known that it admits cut elimination.
\begin{theorem}
  The rule $\AXrule$ is derivable in $\MiLL$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:cutElimIMLL}
  The rule $\cutr$ is admissible $\MiLL$.
\end{theorem}

