\autoref{tab: dfl results} reports the results of the various DFL-based models. For the two smaller NN architectures (NN1 \& NN2), the three DFL methods---i.e., SS, QP-relaxation (QP), and QP subproblem with fixed binaries (FB)---converge successfully. For the most complex architecture, NN3, only QP training is feasible within 24 hours. Indeed, the complexity of the HVAC MS increases with the number of neurons. Consequently, since the SS and FB require to solve the MIQP for each sample, the training time quickly becomes computationally intensive.

The first two rows, \textit{Ex-post+} and Hierarchical Loss, are the metrics used for training. SS can directly minimize \textit{Ex-post+} (i.e., ex-post power cost, thermal discomfort penalty, and power cost misestimation), whereas QP and FB methods minimize the error on the HVAC power through the hierarchical loss \eqref{eq: loss function}. 

SS obtains the best ex-post value compared to QP, and FB.
For NN1, SS (\(\sigma=0.01\)) obtains the lowest \textit{Ex-post+} at \$90. FB and QP follow at \$101 and \$187, respectively. All three NN1 models have an ex-post cost between \$40.5 and \$40.9. However, the model accuracy to predict the cost varies widely. SS is the most accurate model with a daily cost underestimation of \$4.0. FB follows closely with an overestimation of \$4.14. QP makes a more significant error at \$9.37.
Regarding the temperature penalties, SS comes first with only \$33 of daily penalty compared to \$43 for FB, and \$58 for QP. The computational time ranges from 80 minutes for FB to 135 minutes for SS.
\newline
For NN2, the same ranking is observed as for NN1. SS (\(\sigma=0.05\)) outperforms with an \textit{Ex-post+} value at \$80. This is even better than \$90 for NN1. FB and QP follow with \$103 (\$101 for NN1) and \$235 (\$187 for NN1). The ex-post cost ranges from \$41.1 for SS to \$42.5 for QP. The cost estimation error is \$2.19 for SS but rises to \$2.34 for FB and \$12.46 for QP. The temperature penalty goes from \$34 for SS to \$56 and \$37 for FB and QP, respectively. In conclusion, SS offers for both architectures the best thermal comfort and estimate of the power cost along with a very competitive ex-post cost and computational burden.

FB outperforms QP on almost all training and ex-post metrics. For NN1, FB has a better hierarchical loss (13.2) and \textit{Ex-post+} (\$101) than QP (hierarchical loss at 13.6 and \textit{Ex-post+} at \$187). Even though FB has a similar ex-post cost (\$40.6 versus \$40.5 for QP), FB has a lower absolute cost error (\$4.14 versus \$9.37 for QP), and provides more thermal comfort (temperature penalty at \$43 versus \$58 for QP). FB takes advantage of NN2’s stronger modeling abilities to improve its training loss (10.0 vs. 13.2 for NN1)), while the performance of QP gets worse (17.2 vs. 13.6 for NN1). However, FB \textit{Ex-post+} is still about \$103 reflecting similar ex-post metrics. These results show (\textit{i}) the inherent misalignment between the hierarchical loss and the ex-post value; (\textit{ii}) the poor quality of the gradient obtained by relaxing the problem.

The poor gradient approximation generated by QP even undermines the performance of larger architectures such as NN3, causing them to underperform relative to smaller SS-trained architectures. For NN3, the QP relaxation is also used for validation. Indeed, FB and SS require to solve the MIQP at each sample. Since the complexity of the HVAC MS grows with the number of neurons, SS and FB become too cumbersome. QP hierarchical loss and \textit{Ex-post+} are better for NN3 than for NN2 going down from 17.2 to 13.8, and from \$235 to \$92. The ex-post cost stands at \$43, slightly above the usual range. The cost overestimation (\$2.52) and the temperature penalty (\$43) become competitive. However, such a large NN with ten ReLU is long to train (almost four hours) and is still outperformed in terms of \textit{Ex-post+} value by NN1 trained by SS. Moreover, solving the MIQP with NN3 at test time is, at least, ten times longer than with NN1 or NN2.

In contrast, since the RC model is linear, the resulting HVAC MS is inherently a continuous QP problem. Nevertheless, the RC model cannot capture the complexity of the thermal dynamics and exhibits the worst metrics among all models. The RC model is trained to minimize the hierarchical loss, which converges at 29.4 and \textit{Ex-post+} at \$268. Regarding the ex-post metrics, the average daily ex-post cost of HVAC power is \$38.5, but it is largely underestimated at \$31.9. The average daily temperature penalty, reflecting the thermal discomfort, is \$187. Therefore, the ex-post cost is the lowest of all models because the RC model fails to provide comfortable temperatures. The total computational time for training, validation, and test is about three hours.

In conclusion, SS outperforms DFL approaches that differentiate through the optimization problem (QP or FB), particularly when the underlying system or its simulator is non-differentiable. Unlike QP and FB, SS bypasses both the differentiation of the optimization layer and the system model, allowing the direct use of the true ex-post value (\textit{Ex-post+}) as a training signal instead of relying on surrogate task losses. This results in superior gradient estimates and more informative training loss, enabling smaller models to outperform larger QP-trained counterparts while reducing computational overhead at inference.

