\section{Conclusion}

We presented a method to determine the relative position of a pair of conics, for the two following cases:
 a parabola and an ellipse, and a hyperbola and an ellipse. The method requires the computation of certain coefficients which can be calculated directly from the coefficients of the implicit equations of both conics. Among those coefficients is, for example, the discriminant of the characteristic equation of the pair of conics. The sequence of signs of those coefficients characterizes the relative position of the conics. There are 9 relative positions for the pair parabola-ellipse, and 11 relative positions for the pair hyperbola-ellipse.

The method avoids the computation of the intersection points of the conics, and the computation of the distance between the conics. This approach allows working with conics whose implicit equation coefficients depend on a parameter. This may be very useful for application to situations with moving conics.


