In order to prove the convergence of moments, we need to study, for any fixed $p\geq 0$, the asymptotics of the coefficients of the derivative:
$$
H_p \equiv H_p(t):= \left.\frac{d^p}{d^ps}  H(1)\right|_{s=1}.
$$
In order to do this, we will study the behaviour of each of these series, now viewed as analytic functions of $t$, close to their dominant singularity.
In fact, we will prefer to work again in the variable $z$. Moreover, in order to study the successive derivatives $H_p$ at $s=1$, we will make use of the very general fact that algebraic functions are D-finite, meaning that their coefficients as power series are solution of linear recurrence equations. Applying this general fact (to derivatives at the point $s=1$ with $z$ a spectator variable) will give us a very convenient, and essentially automatic, way to study the successive derivatives $H_p$ and their singular behaviour\footnote{we have not sure to have already seen this technique used in this way. That might be the main technical innovation of the present paper.}

The generating function $F(1)$ has a unique singularity for $t=27/256$, which corresponds to the point $z=1/4$. In the sake of uniformization, we will introduce the variable 
$\delta$ related to $z$ by 
\begin{align}\label{eq:delta}
	z=\frac{1}{4}-\delta^2.
\end{align}

The equation~\eqref{eq:Hsexplicit} implies that $H(1)$ is an algebraic function of $z$ and $s$. Its minimal polynomial can be explictly computed easily~\cite{maple}. This gives us an explicit polynomial such that
\begin{align}\label{eq:algH}
B(H(1),z,s)=1.
\end{align}
This polynomial has degree 3 in $H(1)$ and (only) 42 terms.


I follows by induction, differentiating~\eqref{eq:algH}, that for any $p\geq 0$, the derivative
$$
\left(\frac{d}{ds}\right)^p H(1)
$$
can be written as a rational function in $z,s,H(1)$ with denominator $B'_1(H(1),z,s)^p$.


