\subsection{Case 3: Content-centric FAS HetNets} \label{subsec:CCN}
Recent advancements have further introduced FAS in HetNet scenarios, particularly within the content-centric networks (CCNs) \cite{F3_rostami2024cache}. In CCNs, frequently accessed content is cached at small-cell BSs (SBSs) and/or edge nodes \cite{F2_bastug2014living}. This mechanism effectively reduces end-to-end latency and alleviates backhaul load, which is essential for future 6G networks that demand ultra-low latency and high throughput with minimal infrastructural overhead. As shown in Fig. \ref{Fig_CCN}, content caching in CCN-enabled HetNets ensures that data is closer to end-users, minimizing redundant core network transmissions.

