\begin{abstract}
% Highlight the motivation (DDI challenges, multimodal integration difficulty)
Understanding the interaction between different drugs (drug-drug interaction or DDI) is critical for ensuring patient safety and optimizing therapeutic outcomes.
% Existing DDI datasets, however, primarily focus on textual information and largely ignore important insights from multimodal data that capture the intricate mechanisms of drug interactions. 
Existing DDI datasets primarily focus on textual information, overlooking multimodal data that reflect complex drug mechanisms.  
% Introduce MUDI as a novel multimodal dataset focusing on pharmacodynamic DDI
% In this paper, we (1) introduce MUDI, a large-scale \textbf{M}ultimodal biomedical dataset for \textbf{U}nderstanding pharmacodynamic \textbf{D}rug-drug \textbf{I}nteractions and (2) benchmark effective machine learning methods to study it.
In this paper, we (1) introduce MUDI, a large-scale \textbf{M}ultimodal biomedical dataset for \textbf{U}nderstanding pharmacodynamic \textbf{D}rug-drug \textbf{I}nteractions, and (2) benchmark learning methods to study it.  
% In brief, MUDI provides a comprehensive multimodal representation of drugs by combining textual pharmacological descriptions, chemical formulas, molecular structure graphs, and images.
% Mention multi-modalities included: text, molecular graphs, chemical structure images
% Specifically, it contains 310,532 annotated drug pairs with pharmacodynamic interaction labels: Synergism, Antagonism, and New Effect.
In brief, MUDI provides a comprehensive multimodal representation of drugs by combining pharmacological text, chemical formulas, molecular structure graphs, and images across 310,532 annotated drug pairs labeled as Synergism, Antagonism, or New Effect.  
Crucially, to effectively evaluate machine-learning based generalization, MUDI consists of unseen drug pairs in the test set. 
% State baseline results across different fusion strategies
% To evaluate the efficacy of MUDI, we consider benchmark models, including late fusion voting and intermediate fusion strategies.
We evaluate benchmark models using both late fusion voting and intermediate fusion strategies.  
% Finally, we release the MUDI dataset, annotation guidelines, evaluation scripts, and baseline implementations under an open research license.
All data, annotations, evaluation scripts, and baselines are released under an open research license.
% Emphasize potential impact on biomedical and AI communities
%MUDI offers a valuable resource to advance multimodal learning research in biomedical domains, bridging a critical gap between computational methods and real-world pharmacodynamic understanding.
\end{abstract}

