\section{Introduction}
% Motivation: importance of DDI prediction, challenges of multimodal DDI modeling
% Polypharmacy, the simultaneous use of several medications by patients, may give rise to interactions between drugs (drug-drug interactions or DDIs), where one drug alters the effect of another, potentially reducing efficacy or causing adverse events~\cite{mcquade2021drug}. Polypharmacy is common when treating complex diseases and acute conditions or for dealing with elderly patients with comorbidities.
Polypharmacy, the concurrent use of multiple medications, is common in treating complex diseases or comorbid conditions, especially in elderly patients.  
It can lead to drug-drug interactions (DDIs), where one drug alters the effect of another, potentially reducing efficacy or causing adverse events~\cite{mcquade2021drug}.
% 
% A powerful way to handle DDI is to predict beforehand (when assigning drugs), whether it exists, and, if so, the effect of it. This will improve patient safety, optimize treatment strategies, and reduce healthcare costs.
% But to effectively deliver these tasks, one needs two central elements: a suitable predictive method, and a targeted dataset to train and test the method.
A promising approach is to predict DDIs proactively when assigning drugs, improving patient safety and treatment outcomes while reducing healthcare costs.  
To do so effectively requires two key components: a suitable predictive method and a well-targeted dataset for training and evaluation.

%Accurate prediction of DDIs is therefore crucial for ensuring patient safety, optimizing treatment regimens, and reducing healthcare costs.

% Gap in existing datasets and approaches
% Designing a targeted dataset for studying DDI must embrace the multimodal nature of the interactions between medications.
% Firstly, DDI has a diverse range of pharmacological foundations, including chemical properties, pharmacological description, and molecular structure.
% When making DDI prediction, these factors not only offer complementary biological and clinical insights, but also predictive power~\cite{asada2021using}.
Designing such a dataset must reflect the inherently multimodal nature of DDIs, which arise from diverse pharmacological foundations, including chemical properties, pharmacological description, and molecular structure~\cite{asada2021using}.  
% Nevertheless, most existing studies heavily rely on one of these modalities, typically textual data~\cite{zhao2016syntax, sahu2018drug, zhu2020extracting}.
% Such restricted reliance, therefore, limits machine learning models (even the state-of-the-art models)'s ability to fully capture the complex biochemical and pharmacodynamic mechanisms underlying DDI.
Yet most existing datasets focus narrowly on one of these modalities, typically textual data~\cite{zhao2016syntax, sahu2018drug, zhu2020extracting}, limiting models' ability to capture complex biochemical interactions.
% While some recent studies explore more diverse inputs and have made important contributions to multimodal-based DDI prediction, these efforts remain limited as they rely on fragmented, non-standardized data sources.
% Additionally, the multimodal data they consider are still relatively restrictive, as most existing multimodal DDI datasets are either incomplete (in modality coverage) or suffer from inconsistent or missing labels (e.g., labels that indicate interactions).
Some recent multimodal DDI studies address this, but often rely on fragmented or non-standardized sources, with limited modality coverage or label inconsistency.
For example, the state-of-the-art datasets, such as DDInter~\cite{xiong2022ddinter}, simply merge pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic interactions into a single label set and ignore interaction directionality.
These lines of evidence suggest a need for a comprehensive, well-curated multimodal dataset.

% \textcolor{red}{Highlight your contribution is not only the dataset, but also the benchmarking of it!}

% What MUDI contributes: modern, multimodal, pharmacodynamic focused, new unseen drugs in test set
To address these gaps, we introduce \textbf{MUDI} -- a \textbf{M}ultimodal Biomedical Dataset for \textbf{U}nderstanding Pharmacodynamic \textbf{D}rug-Drug \textbf{I}nteractions. 
MUDI is a large-scale, richly annotated collection of paired drugs, integrating multiple data modalities: drug descriptions, molecular structure graphs, molecular structure images, and chemical formulas.
% Unlike existing datasets, MUDI focuses on pharmacodynamic interactions, categorizing them into directed labels (e.g., synergism, antagonism), as well as the undirected label (e.g., new effect).
Unlike existing datasets, MUDI provides directed labels (e.g., Synergism, Antagonism) and undirected labels (e.g., New Effect).  
% Notably, MUDI's test set contains a substantial portion of drug pairs involving previously unseen drugs, enabling rigorous evaluation of model generalization to novel compounds.
Notably, MUDI's test set contains a substantial portion of interactions involving unseen drugs to assess model generalization.
Moreover, we provide multimodal baselines with intermediate and late fusion to benchmark learning from heterogeneous biomedical inputs.
Finally, we release the full MUDI dataset, annotation guidelines, baseline implementations, and benchmarking results publicly under an open license. % to promote transparent and accelerated research in multimodal biomedical AI.

% Summary of contributions
We summarize our key contributions as follows:
% 
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item We construct and release \textbf{MUDI}, a multimodal pharmacodynamic DDI dataset, curated based on clinical knowledge, containing $310{,}532$ annotated drug pairs.
    % \item We provide baseline model and experimental results to benchmark machine learning methods for predicting DDI using MUDI.
    \item We provide benchmark results using \textbf{multimodal baselines} for predicting DDIs from heterogeneous biomedical inputs.
    % \item We publicly release MUDI and benchmark machine learning methods on DDI prediction using MUDI.
    % We discuss licensing, dataset accessibility, reproducibility setups, and ethical considerations related to MUDI to facilitate its responsible and transparent uses.
    \item We publicly release the dataset, annotations, code, and evaluation pipelines to support reproducible multimodal research.
\end{itemize}


