We train six modality-specific models: (i) \texttt{name}, (ii) \texttt{description} (merged from summary, pharmacodynamics, mechanism, metabolism, and indication), (iii) \texttt{formula}, (iv) \texttt{SMILES} (text), (v) molecular structure \texttt{graph}, and (vi) \texttt{image}.
% 
% For \texttt{\textbf{text-based}} modalities (\texttt{name}, \texttt{description}, \texttt{SMILES} (text), \texttt{formula}), we fine-tune BioMedBERT~\cite{gu2021domain}, extracting the final \texttt{[CLS]} token embedding as the modality vector.
For \textbf{text-based} inputs (\texttt{name}, \texttt{description}, \texttt{SMILES}, \texttt{formula}), we fine-tune BioMedBERT~\cite{gu2021domain}, using the final \texttt{[CLS]} token as the drug embedding.
% The \texttt{\textbf{graph}} modality is encoded using a graph convolutional network~\cite{kipf2017semi}, with max pooling applied to obtain an embedding vector.
\textbf{Graph} inputs are processed using a graph convolutional network~\cite{kipf2017semi} with max pooling.
\texttt{\textbf{Image}} inputs are encoded using a Vision Transformer~\cite{dosovitskiy2021an}.
% 
% Each single-modality model predicts interaction labels independently by combining the embeddings of the two drugs through concatenation followed by a shallow classifier.
Each single-modality model concatenates two drug embeddings and feeds them to a shallow classifier.

\vspace{-.1cm}
\paragraph{Late Fusion Baseline}
We construct a late fusion model that aggregates predictions from six single-modality classifiers.
The final prediction is computed via majority voting across the six classifiers, with tie-breaking handled in a fixed modality priority order.
This baseline reflects ensembling without joint feature modeling.

\paragraph{Intermediate Fusion Baseline}
We construct an intermediate fusion baseline by concatenating the modality-specific embeddings into an interleaved sequence.
The resulting fused vector is passed through a two-layer MLP classifier.
% This architecture enables early interaction across modalities while preserving drug pair structure.
This captures cross-modal interactions while preserving pairwise structure.

