\begin{figure*}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{fig2_introduction2.png}
    \caption{\textbf{Overview of the long-tail imbalance in \textsc{ReDial}, its key challenges, and our mitigation strategy.}
    (A) Head films ($\geq 6$ mentions) form only $10.7\%$ of titles but draw $48.4\%$ of mentions; Body ($2$--$5$) add $19.3\%/25.4\%$, while the Tail (single-mention, $\sim70\%$ of titles) receives just $26.3\%$. 
    (B) Head samples drive over-fitting, body samples experience feature drift, and tail samples are marginalised by the skewed distribution. 
    (C) Adaptive Comprehensive Focal Loss curbs head over-fitting; prototype learning stabilises body features for long-tail recommendation; GPT-4o dialogue augmentation enriches tail supervision.
    }
    \label{Introduction2}
\end{figure*}
Conversational Recommender Systems~(CRSs) are a multi\hyp turn, natural\hyp language recommendation framework that dynamically captures users’ needs and preferences, delivering personalized and efficient recommendations \cite{jung2023towards}. A typical CRS task consists of two core components: (i) Item recommendation: Recommend identifying recommendation intent from user input and generating appropriate item suggestions \cite{li2022user,ma2020cr}. (ii) Dialogue generation: Generate producing natural, humanlike responses to improve the
interaction experience and user satisfaction \cite{qian2023hutcrs,wu2022state}.


In recent years, research on CRSs has progressed from context only preference modeling to approaches that incorporate external knowledge, affective cues, and pretrained language models \cite{bizer2009dbpedia}. Early methods modeled user preferences chiefly by analyzing the utterances in a dialogue  \cite{lu2021revcore}. However, because conversational texts are generally short and information--sparse, these systems often defaulted to recommending popular or generic items, making true personalization difficult \cite{chen2019towards}. 
To enrich preference modeling, later work introduced a variety of external information sources----such as knowledge graphs \cite{speer2017conceptnet}, hypergraph structures \cite{zheng2024hycorec}, product reviews \cite{zhou2022c2}, metadata \cite{yang2021improving}, and entity information extracted from similar conversations \cite{dao2024broadening}----to strengthen the system's understanding of user needs.  
At the same time, the reasoning and semantic capabilities of large language models~(LLMs) have been leveraged to improve recommendation accuracy and contextual relevance \cite{zhou2020improving,wang2022towards}. Meanwhile, tightening privacy regulations are accelerating a shift toward federated learning paradigms that keep conversational logs on-device while only exchanging lightweight model updates. Early CRS adaptations—such as the communication-efficient FedRL \cite{di2024fedrl} and the diffusion-guided denoising recommender \cite{di2025federated}—already match centralized baselines without sacrificing user privacy.
Researchers have also begun to model the emotions and attitudes expressed by users, using multidimensional affective cues and preference labels to build finer--grained user representations and further enhance recommendation quality \cite{xi2024memocrs,xu2021adapting,zhao2023multi}.
Methodologically, the field has shifted from early entity--level preference extraction to holistic semantic modeling, employing pretrained architectures such as Transformer and RoBERTa to capture richer contextual features; representative systems include \mbox{C$^{2}$-CRS} \cite{zhou2022c2}, UniCRS \cite{wang2022towards}, and DCRS \cite{christakopoulou2016towards}, which markedly improve the understanding and response capability for complex dialogues.

Despite the rapid progress of CRSs, serious limitations persist.\@ 
Taking \textsc{DCRS} as a representative model, we scrutinized its recommendations and uncovered several recurring failure cases.\@ 
As illustrated in Figure~\ref{introduction1}A, \textit{Case~1} involves a user seeking fast-paced crime thrillers, whereas \textit{Case~2} depicts a user requesting delicate and emotionally rich dramas.\@ 
Nevertheless, Figure~\ref{introduction1}B shows that \textsc{DCRS} recommends the same mainstream blockbuster---\textit{Inception}---to both conversations.\@ 
This behaviour reflects a pronounced \emph{popularity bias}: even when users articulate niche preferences, the model falls back on head-frequency items and neglects the long tail, revealing inadequate modelling of low-frequency entities.
This suggests that, even with explicit niche interests, the model avoids low-frequency items and thus lacks effective long-tail modelling. 
To diagnose the origin of this popularity bias, we inspected the \textsc{ReDial} dataset and uncovered an extreme long-tail distribution (Fig.~\ref{Introduction2}A).
Using mention frequency, we partitioned the movies into three segments—Head, Body, and Tail—and compared each segment’s coverage in the dialogue corpus.
Although head films constitute only 10.7\,\% of the catalogue, they receive 48.4\,\% of all mentions. 
Body films cover a further 19.3\,\% of titles but attract just 25.4\,\% of mentions, while the tail---nearly 70\,\% of the inventory---is discussed in a mere 26.3\,\% of turns. 
Blockbusters such as Titanic therefore dominate the conversational space, while acclaimed niche works like Isle of Dogs are seldom surfaced.
This pronounced imbalance deprives the model of tail-item supervision, weakens its ability to capture fine-grained long-tail preferences, and ultimately curbs recommendation diversity and genuine personalisation.

The inherent data imbalance of long-tail distributions poses severe challenges to the two core tasks of CRSs—item recommendation and dialogue generation (see Fig.~\ref{Introduction2}B). To clarify the impact, we divide movies into three tiers according to their popularity:  
(i) \textbf{Head movies} have abundant user interactions, causing the model to overfit high-frequency samples and bias recommendations toward popular content;  
(ii) \textbf{Body movies} are often marginalized, leading to unstable representations and volatile recommendation performance;  
(iii) \textbf{Tail movies} suffer from extreme sparsity and pronounced train–test distribution shift, which exacerbate the cold-start problem and hinder the system from capturing and expressing niche preferences.  
These three issues compound and propagate along the recommendation pipeline, so that in the dialogue generation stage the system’s responses rely excessively on head-dominated content, lacking diversity and personalization. Consequently, the overall balance among accuracy, diversity, and fairness deteriorates.

To alleviate the imbalance, we design tier specific solutions for head, body, and tail movies (Fig.~\ref{Introduction2}C):
(i) \textbf{Head movies.} An Adaptive Composite Focused Loss (ACFL) dynamically tunes class weights and focusing factors to curb overfitting on high frequency samples and reduce popularity bias. 
(ii) \textbf{Body movies.} A prototype based representation framework selects prototypes via semantic, affective, and contextual similarity, guiding clearer feature clustering and more stable representations.  
(iii) \textbf{Tail movies.} A \emph{GPT4o–driven dialogue level augmentation} generates semantically equivalent snippets from tail prototypes and validates them with an automatic–manual pipeline, enriching sparse data.
Acting at the \emph{loss}, \emph{representation}, and \emph{data} layers, these strategies jointly improve long\hyp tail recommendation, diversity, and fairness. In practice (Fig.~\ref{introduction1}C), the system offers more precise suggestions: it proposes the Korean thriller \emph{A~Hard~Day} for a crime\hyp film seeker and recommends niche animation \emph{Isle~of~Dogs} for an art\hyp drama lover, illustrating reduced popularity bias and better intent alignment.

The main contributions of our work are summarized as follows:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item 
    To suppress head movie overfitting and popularity bias, we introduce ACFL, a dynamic loss function that rebalances training by down-weighting high frequency samples.

    \item
    To mitigate representation instability in mid-frequency movies, we propose a prototype-based method that clusters samples by semantic, emotional, and movie-level similarity to improve representation quality and discrimination.

    \item  
    To tackle sparsity and distributional drift in tail movies, we propose a prototype-centered augmentation pipeline that leverages LLMs to generate diverse dialogues, enhancing both coverage and cold-start resilience.
    \item 
    On the \textsc{ReDial} and \textsc{INSPIRED} datasets, \textsc{LumiCRS} sets new SOTA across all three tasks:  
Recall@10 improves by around 6.7\%, Tail-Recall@10 by around 10\%, Coverage@10 and ILD@10 by around 9\%, and Distinct-2 by around 10.5\%.  
In addition, with our newly proposed subjective evaluation metrics for conversational generation, \textsc{LumiCRS} also achieves significant improvements.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Related Work}
\subsection{CRS technological evolution.}  
Conversational recommender systems (CRS) generate personalised suggestions through multi–turn natural language interaction, typically comprising a \emph{dialogue module} for intent understanding and response generation and a \emph{recommendation module} that exploits conversational context.  The research trajectory has progressed from template‑based retrieval (MovieLens‑Dial \cite{harper2015movielens}) to hybrid HRED collaborative filtering architectures (ReDial\cite{li2018towards}), and subsequently to knowledge‑enhanced models such as KBRD\cite{chen2019towards} and KGSF \cite{zhou2020improving}.  Reinforcement‑learning frameworks (CRM \cite{lei2020conversational}, EAR \cite{lei2020estimation}) further refine the ask‑vs‑recommend strategy.  Most recently, large language models (LLMs) have enabled end to end unification: UniCRS\cite{wang2022towards}, GUS \cite{shi2025unifiedgenerativesearchrecommendation} and G‑CRS \cite{qiu2025graphretrievalaugmentedllmconversational} markedly improve conversational fluency and recommendation accuracy.
\medskip
\subsection{Long‑tail bias in movie recommendation.}  
Movie datasets exhibit a pronounced long‑tail distribution: roughly 10\,\% of head movies account for 50–60\,\% of interactions, whereas 70\,\% of tail movies receive almost no ratings (MovieLens‑1M, Netflix, TGReDial \cite{Zhou2021TGReDial}).  This \emph{popularity bias} reduces diversity, exacerbates cold‑start and fairness issues \cite{Abdollahpouri2019,Steck2019Popularity,Burke2020Fairness}.  Existing remedies include post‑hoc re‑ranking (xQuad \cite{Santos2020xQuad}, MM‑Rec \cite{Liu2021MMRec}, LAP‑SR \cite{Zhang2024LAPSR}), loss re‑weighting or regularisation (Pop‑MF \cite{Chen2020PopMF}, BPR+IRR \cite{Zhu2023IRR}, CausE \cite{Zhang2020CausE}), and data augmentation (SimRec \cite{Gao2023SimRec}, ConvoAug \cite{Li2024ConvoAug}, LLM‑Aug \cite{Zhou2024LLMAug}); industrial traffic reallocation (Repurpose‑Rec \cite{Smith2024Repurpose}) shows additional promise.  Nevertheless, these methods are usually designed for static recommenders or single‑strategy pipelines and have yet to form a holistic CRS solution.

\medskip
\subsection{Prototype learning for long‑tail CRS.}  
Prototype learning, originating from few‑shot classification, learns representative embeddings that remain discriminative under extreme data sparsity.  In recommendation, ProtoCF \cite{Sankar2021ProtoCF} applies meta‑learning and knowledge distillation to boost tail‑item recall by 60–80\,\%; TIPR \cite{Wang2023TIPR} and CL4Rec \cite{Wei2022CL4Rec} introduce contrastive or multi‑centre mechanisms, while ProtoGCD \cite{Liu2025ProtoGCD} achieves new SOTA with a global–local prototype hierarchy.  However, these studies target offline settings and ignore conversational context.  To bridge this gap, we integrate \emph{prototype representations}, an \emph{adaptive loss} and \emph{dialogue‑level augmentation} into a unified CRS, applying differentiated strategies to head, body and tail segments.  The three components act on the representation, optimisation and data layers, respectively, collaboratively mitigating popularity bias and substantially improving coverage, accuracy and diversity for long‑tail movie recommendation.
\section{Preliminaries}
We denote the item set as \( I = \{i_1, i_2, \dots, i_N\} \), and the set of conversational contexts as \( S = \{s_1, s_2, \dots, s_M\} \). From the conversations in \( S \), we extract all mentioned entities to form the entity set \( E = \{e_1, e_2, \dots, e_K\} \), where \( I \subseteq E \). Here, \( N \) denotes the number of items, \( M \) the number of conversational contexts, and \( K \) the total number of entities.

Each conversational context \( s \in S \) consists of a sequence of utterances, denoted as:
\[
s = \{c_b\}_{b=1}^n.
\]
At the \( b \)-th turn, each utterance \( c_b \) is a sequence of words:
\[
c_b = \{w_j\}_{j=1}^m,
\]
where \( W \) denotes the vocabulary set.

As the conversation progresses, utterances are aggregated into a dialogue history. A Conversational Recommender System (CRS) leverages this history to infer user preferences and generate appropriate responses. At turn \( b \), the recommender module selects a set of candidate items from \( I \) based on the inferred preferences, while the dialogue module generates the next system utterance \( c_b \) based on the prior context.

\section{Methodology}
This section outlines our long-tail mitigation strategy across head, mid-tail, and tail items. Section 4.1 introduces an adaptive composite focused loss to curb head-item overfitting, whereas Sections 4.2 and 4.3 present a prototype-based representation framework and a GPT-4o-Based Dialogue Augmentation scheme to strengthen modeling of mid-tail and tail items, respectively. 
\subsection{Adaptive Comprehensive Focal Loss (ACFL)}
To combat the severe class imbalance and head-item overfitting induced by the long-tail phenomenon, we propose the Adaptive Comprehensive Focal Loss (ACFL). ACFL unifies class-frequency weighting, dynamic focusing factors, Top-K hard-example mining, and adaptive sampling within a single framework, thereby up-weighting tail samples, suppressing redundant high-frequency signals, and concentrating on hard instances during training. These mechanisms jointly mitigate popularity bias and substantially enhance both the accuracy and diversity of recommendations for long-tail items. This section explicates the design principles of ACFL, provides its complete mathematical formulation, and benchmarks it against recent state-of-the-art loss functions for long-tail recommendation and imbalanced classification.
\subsubsection{ACFL Formulation and Components}

We propose the Adaptive Comprehensive Focal Loss (ACFL), which substantially enhances the standard Focal Loss (FL) by addressing its inherent limitations. The original formulation of FL is as follows:
\begin{equation}
\text{FL}(p_t) = -\alpha_t (1 - p_t)^\gamma \log(p_t),
\end{equation}
where $p_t$ is the predicted probability of the true class, $\alpha_t$ denotes the class balance factor, and $\gamma$ is the focusing parameter controlling the down-weighting rate for easy examples. While effective, standard FL suffers from fixed hyperparameters, inadequate class imbalance consideration, and limited special handling of challenging samples.

To overcome these limitations, ACFL integrates four key mechanisms: adaptive class weighting, adaptive balancing and focusing factors, a Top-K selection strategy, and adaptive sampling. Specifically:

\textbf{Adaptive Class Weighting}: Class weights $w_c$ are dynamically adjusted according to class frequencies during training:
\begin{equation}
w_c = \frac{1}{N_c + \epsilon},
\end{equation}
where $N_c$ is the number of samples in class $c$, and $\epsilon$ is a smoothing factor.

\textbf{Adaptive Balance and Focus Factors}: Unlike standard FL with fixed parameters, ACFL dynamically adjusts the balance factor $\alpha_t$ and the focusing parameter $\gamma_t$ based on the difficulty of individual samples:
\begin{align}
\alpha_t &= \text{clip}(\alpha p_t + \epsilon, \alpha_{\min}, \alpha_{\max}), \\
\gamma_t &= \text{clip}(\gamma (1 - p_t), \gamma_{\min}, \gamma_{\max}).
\end{align}
These adjustments ensure that harder samples receive greater attention during model optimization.

\textbf{Top-K Hard Example Selection}: To further emphasize hard examples, ACFL employs a Top-K strategy that calculates the loss only for the most challenging samples. Given the predicted probability distribution, a threshold $\tau_k$ at the $k$-th percentile is used to define a binary mask $M_i$:
\begin{equation}
\tau_k = \text{quantile}(p_i, k), \quad M_i = \mathbf{1}\{p_i \geq \tau_k\}.
\end{equation}

\textbf{Adaptive Sampling Strategy}: An adaptive sampling method adjusts sampling weights based on class frequencies:
\begin{equation}
w_{\text{sample}, c} = \begin{cases}
1 + \frac{1}{N_c}, & N_c < \theta_{\min} \quad \text{(oversampling)}, \\
1 - \frac{N_c}{N_{\max}}, & N_c > \theta_{\max} \quad \text{(undersampling)}, \\
1, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
\begin{algorithm}
\caption{ACFL: Forward Pass and Weighting}
\label{alg:acfl_forward}
\KwIn{Training samples $\{(x_j, y_j)\}_{j=1}^{N}$; predicted probability $p_j = \hat{y}_j$; popularity score $pop(x_j)$; hyperparameters $\alpha$, $\gamma$, $\beta$}
\KwOut{Sample-wise weighted loss values $l_j$}
\For{$j \gets 1$ \KwTo $N$}{
    Compute focal factor: $w_j \gets \alpha \cdot (1 - p_j)^{\gamma}$\;
    Compute adaptive weight: $a_j \gets \exp(-\beta \cdot pop(x_j))$\;
    Compute weighted sample loss:\;
    \quad $l_j \gets -a_j \cdot w_j \cdot \left[ y_j \log(p_j) + (1 - y_j) \log(1 - p_j) \right]$\;
}
\Return $\{l_j\}_{j=1}^{N}$
\end{algorithm}

\vspace{1em}

\begin{algorithm}
\caption{ACFL: Loss Aggregation}
\label{alg:acfl_aggregate}
\KwIn{Sample losses $\{l_j\}_{j=1}^{N}$}
\KwOut{Total loss $\mathcal{L}_{\text{ACFL}}$}
Initialize total loss: $\mathcal{L}_{\text{ACFL}} \gets 0$\;

\For{$j \gets 1$ \KwTo $N$}{
    $\mathcal{L}_{\text{ACFL}} \gets \mathcal{L}_{\text{ACFL}} + l_j$\;
}
Normalize: $\mathcal{L}_{\text{ACFL}} \gets \frac{1}{N} \cdot \mathcal{L}_{\text{ACFL}}$\;

\Return $\mathcal{L}_{\text{ACFL}}$
\end{algorithm}
\subsubsection{Comprehensiveness Verification of ACFL Design}
To rigorously evaluate the completeness and robustness of \textbf{ACFL} in long-tail recommendation, we benchmark it against six recent loss functions—Adaptive Logit Adjustment (ALA)~\cite{ALA2024}, Enhanced Adaptive Focal Loss (EAFL)~\cite{EAFL2024}, Asymmetric Loss with Padé Approximation (ALPA)~\cite{ALPA2025}, Cost-Sensitive Loss (CSL)~\cite{CSL2024}, Distributionally Robust Loss (DR-Loss)~\cite{DRLoss2024}, and Adaptive Hierarchical Loss (AHL)~\cite{AHL2025}. 
The comparison focuses on seven capabilities that are critical for modelling extreme long-tail distributions: \ding{172} class weighting: attenuating head-class dominance at the loss level, \ding{173} hard-sample emphasis: amplifying tail-instance gradients, \ding{174} adaptive parameter tuning: dynamically adjusting key factors during training, \ding{175} dynamic sampling: resampling to curb head–tail data bias, \ding{176} Top-$K$ hard-example mining: prioritising the most informative tail cases, \ding{177} imbalance suitability: design premised on extreme class skew, and \ding{178} manual-free hyper-parameter tuning: avoiding costly human search in production. 

