\subsection{Baseline}
Following the survey and benchmark protocol of \citet{mb35}, we compare our model with 15 representative conversational recommender (CRS) baselines that span four technical streams: statistical ranking, pre-trained language models (PLMs), knowledge-graph (KG)–enhanced systems, and retrieval / disentangled or multi-strategy frameworks. All implementations and hyper-parameters are reproduced from public code to ensure a fair comparison.

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \underline{\textbf{Popularity}}: It ranks movies purely by their historical occurrence frequency, providing a lower-bound reference for recommendation.

    \item \underline{\textbf{TextCNN}} \cite{mb39}:  It applies convolutional neural networks to capture local contextual features in dialogue and ranks candidate movies accordingly.

    \item \underline{\textbf{BERT}} \cite{mb40}:  It fine-tunes a bi-directional Transformer on dialogue context to predict a shortlist of likely movies.

    \item \underline{\textbf{GPT-2}} \cite{mb41}: It serves as a large-scale autoregressive language-model baseline for both context modelling and text generation.

    \item \underline{\textbf{DialoGPT}} \cite{mb42}: It fine-tunes GPT-2 on extensive conversational data to generate fluent and contextually coherent responses.

    \item \underline{\textbf{BART}} \cite{mb43}: It employs a denoising sequence-to-sequence auto-encoder to support both understanding and generation in dialogue tasks.

    \item \underline{\textbf{ReDial}} \cite{mb28}: It combines an auto-encoder recommender with an HRED generator, forming the earliest English CRS baseline.

    \item \underline{\textbf{KBRD}} \cite{mb24}: It introduces an entity-level knowledge graph to bridge recommendation and response generation via knowledge propagation.

    \item \underline{\textbf{KGSF}} \cite{mb26}: It integrates word-level and entity-level knowledge graphs and aligns them via mutual-information maximisation to enrich representations.

    \item \underline{\textbf{UniCRS}} \cite{wang2022towards}: It unifies recommendation and dialogue subtasks within a prompt-learning paradigm driven by large pre-trained language models.

    \item \underline{\textbf{TREA}} \cite{mb29}:  It constructs a multi-layer inferable tree to perform causal reasoning over entities, improving explainability and response coherence.

    \item \underline{\textbf{VRICR}} \cite{mb31}: It dynamically completes incomplete knowledge graphs using variational Bayesian inference conditioned on dialogue context.

    \item \underline{\textbf{DCRS}} \cite{mb35}: It retrieves demonstration dialogues highly similar to the current context to enhance understanding and generation.

    \item \underline{\textbf{DisenCRS}} \cite{disencrs}: It employs an information-bottleneck with contrastive learning to disentangle multiple user-preference facets, boosting long-tail recall and response consistency.

    \item \underline{\textbf{MSCRS}} \cite{mscrs}: It adopts a two-stage retrieve → generate/rewrite strategy to balance recommendation accuracy with conversational diversity.
\end{itemize}


\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}
In conversational recommender systems (CRSs), the workflow is divided into two core tasks—item recommendation and response generation—which correspond to the research questions RQ1 and RQ2 in this paper. To provide a comprehensive assessment of model performance, we establish a systematic set of evaluation metrics for each task, enabling us to quantify overall accuracy while capturing behavior in long-tail scenarios and from a user-experience perspective.

