Table~\ref{tab:case_study_bigdaddy} presents a case from the \textsc{ReDial} corpus in which the user states that they “really liked \textit{Big Daddy}” and requests “another good comedy like that.”  
Four single-turn responses are compared: the platform’s default reply, \textsc{GPT-4o}, \textsc{LLaMA2-Chat}, and our \textsc{LumiCRS}.  
Each answer is assessed on five 0–5 scales—\textit{Fluency}, \textit{Info}, \textit{Persua}, \textit{Human}, and \textit{TailRel}—shown in italics beneath the text.  

The baseline systems concentrate on well-known Adam Sandler titles such as \textit{Happy Gilmore} and \textit{Billy Madison}.  
Although \textsc{GPT-4o} and \textsc{LLaMA2-Chat} provide brief plot summaries and light emotive language, their long-tail relevance remains low ($TailRel \leq 1.5$).  
The default reply is even more limited, offering only \textit{Grown Ups} and scoring poorly on information and human-likeness.

By contrast, LumiCRS opens with an empathic remark (“So glad you liked \textit{Big Daddy} —it’s a classic!”) and recommends two thematically aligned yet much rarer films: the coming-of-age comedy The Way Way Back and the musical dramedy Sing Street.  
This response attains the highest scores in all five dimensions (Fluency 4.3, Info 4.0, Persua 3.9, Human 4.1, TailRel 3.8), demonstrating greater linguistic naturalness, richer justification, stronger personability, and—crucially—a markedly better ability to surface niche content.

These gains arise from \textsc{LumiCRS}’s three-pronged architecture: \textbf{(i)} the prototype-learning module for long-tail recommendation constructs context–affect–semantic prototypes for body and tail movies, stabilising sparse-item representations; \textbf{(ii)} Adaptive Comprehensive Focal Loss (ACFL) attenuates head-tier gradients while amplifying hard tail cases, reducing popularity bias; and \textbf{(iii)} the GPT-4o-based prototype-dialogue augmenter selectively injects diverse, emotionally vivid utterances around tail entities. Acting in concert, these mechanisms enable \textsc{LumiCRS} to produce responses that are informative, engaging, and rich in long-tail recommendations.

\section{Conclusion}
We introduced \textsc{LumiCRS}, a long-tail–aware conversational recommender that unifies three complementary mechanisms: 
\emph{(i)} Adaptive Composite Focal Loss (ACFL) for head-suppression and tail-amplification; 
\emph{(ii)} a prototype-learning module that yields stable context–affect–semantic representations for mid- and low-frequency movies; and 
\emph{(iii)} a GPT-4o-based prototype-dialogue augmentation pipeline that injects diverse, quality-controlled utterances to enrich sparse supervision. 
Comprehensive experiments on the \textbf{ReDial} and \textbf{INSPIRED} benchmarks show that \textsc{LumiCRS} surpasses 15 strong baselines: overall \textbf{Recall@1/10/50} rises from \textit{0.081/0.268/0.451} to \textbf{0.088/0.286/0.495} on ReDial and from \textit{0.096/0.252/0.423} to \textbf{0.104/0.274/0.466} on INSPIRED, delivering 5–10\% relative gains. 
Long-tail discovery improves in parallel, with \textbf{Tail-Recall@50} climbing \textbf{+11.8\%} and \textbf{+12.9\%}, while catalogue coverage and ILD each grow by roughly \textbf{9\%}. 
Dialogue-quality metrics (\textit{e.g.}, BLEU-2/3, ROUGE-L, DIST-4) advance by 3–11\%, and human evaluation confirms superior fluency, informativeness, persuasiveness, and long-tail relevance (\textbf{TailRel} +13.9\%). 
Ablation studies verify that every component is indispensable: removing prototype learning or augmentation lowers Recall@10 by up to 10\%, and replacing ACFL with seven recent imbalance losses reduces BLEU-2 by as much as 36\%. 
These results highlight the effectiveness of unifying optimisation, representation, and data-level strategies for long-tail conversational recommendation.
\section{Limitations \& Future Work}
Despite these gains, LumiCRS still relies on offline hyper-parameter tuning and GPU-intensive prototype updates; sensitivity analysis shows performance drops when key values drift from the mid-range optima. In addition, our evaluation is restricted to the movie domain and to two English benchmarks, leaving cross-domain generalisation and multilingual robustness unexplored. Finally, the framework assumes centralised access to conversational logs and item metadata, an arrangement that conflicts with emerging privacy regulations such as GDPR and CCPA. We therefore plan to (i) automate hyper-parameter search with lightweight population-based training, (ii) distil the prototype learner to reduce GPU demand, (iii) extend the curriculum scheduler to detect domain shifts and re-tune on the fly, and (iv) migrate the entire pipeline to a federated-learning (FL) setting in which raw dialogues stay on-device and only sparse, transformation-filtered updates are exchanged. The fine-grained transformation and hybrid-information-sharing strategy introduced by a recent personalised consumer FL recommender \cite{di2025personalized} offers a practical blueprint for maintaining both privacy and long-tail coverage, and will guide our forthcoming edge-deployment experiments across multiple media domains and languages.





