For  Hamiltonian systems \eqref{semilinear HODE} with  high-order polynomial  function $U(x)$, EKahan method can be generalized using the polarization technique presented in   \cite{MR3452144}. Suppose $U(x)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of order $k+2$, EKahan scheme is given by 
\begin{equation}\label{EKahan scheme-high-order}
x_{n+k}=e^{khQM} x_{n} +kh \phi(khQM)Q\nabla U_{\text{K}}(x_{n},x_{n+1},\cdots,x_{n+k}),
\end{equation}
where $\nabla U_{\text{K}}(\cdot,\cdots,\cdot)$  is a symmetric $k+1$-linear polarized function of $\nabla U(x)$, satisfying $$\nabla U_{\text{K}}(x_n,x_{n+1},\cdots,x_{n+k}) =  \sum_{\substack{1 \leq m \leq k+1 \\ n \leq i_1 < \cdots < i_m \leq n+k}}  \frac{(-1)^{k+1 - m}}{(k+1)!}  \nabla U(x_{i_1} + \cdots + x_{i_m}).
$$
%Here, $\nabla U(x_{n},x_{n+1},\cdots,x_{n+k})$ is a polarization of the true gradient $\nabla U(x)$. 
In this case, we give the following corollaries to describe EKahan's properties.
\begin{corollary}\label{corollary-high-order-symmetric-order}
For semilinear system \eqref{semilinear HODE} with  high-order polynomial  function $U(x)$, the EKahan scheme \eqref{EKahan scheme-high-order} is symmetric and second order if the initial starting points $x_{n+1},\cdots,x_{n+k}$ are given using a at least second-order method.
\end{corollary}
This corollary is a generalization of Lemma \ref{EKahan symmetric Lemma} and \ref{EKahan order Lemma} for Hamiltonian systems \eqref{semilinear HODE} with cubic polynomial function $U(x)$ to systems with high-order polynomial functions, and the proof follows  similarly.
\begin{corollary}\label{corollary-high-order-energy}
For the semilinear system \eqref{semilinear HODE}, any exponential integrator with the form 
\begin{align*}
x_{n+k}=e^{khA} x_{n} +kh \phi(khA)Q\hat{\nabla}U(x_{n},x_{n+1},\cdots,x_{n+k}),
\end{align*}
where $A=QM$, and $\hat{\nabla}U(x_{n},x_{n+1},\cdots,x_{n+k})$ is a discretiztion of the true gradient $\nabla U(x_{n})$, satisfies the following  equation
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{2k}x_{n+k}^\mathsf{T} Mx_{n+k}-\frac{1}{2k}x_{n}^\mathsf{T} Mx_{n}+\frac{1}{k}(x_{n+k}-x_{n})^\mathsf{T} \hat{\nabla}U(x_{n},x_{n+1},\cdots,x_{n+k})=0.
\end{align*}
\end{corollary}
The proof of this corollary follows similarly as the proof of Lemma \ref{lemma-energy}.

\begin{corollary}\label{corollary-EKahan-energy-high-order}
For a homogeneous  function $U(x)$, EKahan scheme \eqref{EKahan scheme-high-order} nearly preserves the exact energy and  has the following error estimation: 
\begin{align*}
H_{n+1}-H_{n}=&\bar{U}(x_{n+1},x_{n+2},\dots,x_{n+k-1},x_{n+k},x_{n+k},x_{n+1}-x_{n})\\
&+\bar{U}(x_{n},x_{n+1},\dots,x_{n+k-2},x_{n+k-1},x_{n},x_{n+k}-x_{n+k-1})\\
&-2\bar{U}(x_{n},x_{n+1},\dots,x_{n+k-1},x_{n+k},\frac{x_{n+k}-x_{n}}{k}),
\end{align*}
where $H_{n}=\frac{1}{2k}\sum\limits_{i=0}^{k-1} x_{n+i}^\mathsf{T} Mx_{n+i}+\bar{U}(x_{n},x_{n+1},\dots,x_{n+k-2},x_{n+k-1},x_{n},x_{n+k-1})$ is the discrete energy at $t=t_n$, and $\bar{U}(\cdot,\cdot,\dots,\cdot,\cdot)$ is a symmetric $k+2$ linear function defined via the $k+2$ tensor associated with the homogeneous function $U(x)$ and satisfies $\bar{U}(x,x,\dots,x,x)=U(x)$.
\end{corollary}
The proof of this corollary proceeds analogously to that of Theorem \ref{theorem-energy}. For polynomial nonlinear functions $U(x)$ of order greater than three, the energy error can no longer be expressed in the simple form presented in Theorem \ref{theorem-energy}; nevertheless, Corollary \ref{corollary-EKahan-energy-high-order} demonstrates that the energy error over a single time step can be effectively controlled by the corresponding change in the numerical solution, thereby ensuring the boundedness of the energy error. 

\begin{remark}
For a nonhomogeneous polynomial $U(x)$, we can extend it to a homogeneous function  by introducing an auxiliary variable $x^0$ to the original vector $x$. Denote the extended variable as $\bar{x} = (x^0, x^1, \cdots, x^m)^\mathsf{T}$, where $x^i$, $i = 1, \cdots, m$, are the components of the vector $x$. The nonhomogeneous cubic polynomial $U(x)$ is extended to the homogeneous function $\bar{U}(x^0, x^1, \cdots, x^m)$ such that $\bar{U}(1, x^1, \cdots, x^m) = U(x)$. Furthermore, to preserve  the structural properties of the original system in the extended formulation, we also augment the skew-symmetric matrix $Q$ to $\bar{Q}$ and the symmetric matrix $M$ to $\bar{M}$ by introducing an extra row and column of zero elements. Notably, $\bar{Q}$ remains skew-symmetric. Furthermore, applying the EKahan method to the modified differential equation
\[
\dot{\bar{x}}(t) = \bar{Q} \bar{M} \bar{x}(t) + \bar{Q} \nabla \bar{U}(\bar{x}(t)), \quad \bar{x}(t_0) = [1, x_0^\mathsf{T}]^\mathsf{T},
\]
produces the same update for $x$ as applying the EKahan method directly to the original reduced system \eqref{semilinear HODE}. Therefore, all results previously established for systems with homogeneous polynomial nonlinearities extend naturally to those involving nonhomogeneous polynomial terms.
\end{remark}


