\begin{lstlisting}[style=Python, label=lst:a_tictactoe, caption={Partial implementation of Tic-Tac-Toc, employing an asynchronous programming model}]

def async_tic_tac_toe(connection):
  board = TicTacToeBoard()
  while not board.is_full():
    action = connection.send_obs_and_wait_for_action(board.obs())
    board.mark_slot(action)
    if board.someone_has_won():
        connection.send_victory(board.get_current_player())
        return
    board.switch_current_player()
  connection.send_draw()
  return
\end{lstlisting}
\end{figure}

This implementation closely resembles a human understanding of the rules of Tic-Tac-Toe, and while it is a perfectly viable design for environments that inherently exist in a separate process (such as 3D simulated environments observed through a video stream), it has significant drawbacks when process separation is unnecessary. In particular, since it cannot run in the same thread as the ML components, communication overheads are introduced.
