\begin{abstract}
% Information retrieval (IR) is a cornerstone of many industrial applications, notably
% In retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) pipelines, the quality of the retrieval model directly impacts downstream performance. 
A limitation of modern document retrieval embedding methods is that they typically encode passages (chunks) from the same documents independently, often overlooking crucial contextual information from the rest of the document that could greatly improve individual chunk representations.

In this work, we introduce \emph{ConTEB} (Context-aware Text Embedding Benchmark), a benchmark designed to evaluate retrieval models on their ability to leverage document-wide context. Our results show that state-of-the-art embedding models struggle in retrieval scenarios where context is required. To address this limitation, we propose \emph{InSeNT} (In-sequence Negative Training), a novel contrastive post-training approach which combined with \textit{late chunking} pooling enhances contextual representation learning while preserving computational efficiency. Our method significantly improves retrieval quality on \emph{ConTEB} without sacrificing base model performance. 
We further find chunks embedded with our method are more robust to suboptimal chunking strategies and larger retrieval corpus sizes.
We open-source all artifacts at \url{https://github.com/illuin-tech/contextual-embeddings}. 

% Neural embedding models are central to modern information retrieval, yet existing evaluations overlook structural priors in real-world corpora. To address this gap, we introduce \textit{\emph{ConTEB}}, the Context-Aware Embedding Leaderboard, a benchmark designed to assess retrieval models in settings where considering contextual cues is key to produce relevant chunk-level text representations. This approach ensures a more realistic evaluation of these models' effectiveness in downstream applications, and demonstrates the limitations of standard embedding models when they are not associated to expensive contextualization strategies in order to overcome their limitations.

% We propose a novel post-training strategy, leveraging late chunking and a custom contrastive loss using \textit{in-sequence negatives}, enabling a better integration of contextualization capabilities into retrieval models. Our approach achieves strong performance on \textit{ConTEB} while maintaining minimal computational overhead, yielding an optimal trade-off between retrieval accuracy and efficiency. We further show our post-training strategy does not degrade the original embedding model performance on standard retrieval tasks, ensuring retro-compatibility with existing systems. 
% We release project artifacts at \url{http://hf.co/<anon>}. 
\end{abstract}


